      "final_text": "我们的观点　7月就业报告疲软之后，8月就业增长出现反弹。近期数据似乎受到两个波动性因素的影响：1）世界杯赛事，推动了休闲与酒店业的波动性；2）地方政府教育领域的季节性扭曲。8月，休闲与酒店业新增就业岗位超出常规，达+62,000个，而地方政府教育新增+42,000个——均为7月数据偏弱后的回补。剔除上述两项因素，就业增长将为+58,000，而非+162,000。尽管如此，8月报告仍有若干积极亮点，尤其是更广泛的U6失业率（涵盖边缘附着劳动者、灰心丧气者及因经济原因从事兼职工作者）下降0.2个百分点至7.7%，创2025年6月以来最低水平。薪资增长目前处于本轮周期的较低水平，接近疫情前的节奏，这意味着劳动力市场并非是过度通胀压力的来源，尤其是在当前高通胀主要由供应因素驱动的背景下。报告的另一积极之处在于制造业（+16,000）和建筑业（+22,000）的就业数据，均高于近期趋势，显示出商品生产部门有所企稳。市场将此次就业报告解读为偏鹰派，收益率和美元走高，而股票则小幅下跌。市场对美联储9月加息的定价已升至约60%。尽管如此，本周的消费者价格指数和生产者价格指数数据对美联储9月决策的影响，很可能大于就业报告本身。美联储官员可能会淡化就业数据的波动性，而更加关注近期通胀信号。",
      "iterations": 0,
      "seg_status": "edited",
      "source_text": "What we think After a weak report in July, jobs growth rebounded in August. Recent data appears to be influenced by two volatile factors: 1) the World Cup, which has driven volatility in leisure & hospitality, and 2) seasonal distortions in local government education. In August, leisure & hospitality added an outsized +62k jobs, while local government education added +42k – both paybacks from weak July prints. Excluding these two, jobs growth would have been +58k rather than +162k. Still, there are some positive features in the August report, especially the -0.2pp drop in the broader U6 unemployment rate (including workers who are marginally attached, discouraged and part-time for economic reasons) to 7.7%, its lowest level since June 2025. Wage growth is now at some of the lowest levels of the cycle and close to the pre-pandemic pace, meaning the labor market is not a source of excess inflationary pressure, especially in a context where elevated inflation is driven mostly by supply-side factors. Another positive part of the report was the numbers for both the manufacturing (+16K) and construction (+22K) sectors. These prints are above recent trends and illustrate some firming in goods producing sectors. Markets interpreted the jobs report as hawkish with yields and the dollar up while equities sold off modestly. Market pricing of a September Fed hike has now risen to around 60%. That said, this week's CPI and PPI data likely matter far more for the September Fed decision than the jobs report. Fed officials will likely discount the jobs volatility and place more emphasis on near-term inflation signals.",
